body: "Source: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1456\n\n## Problem\n`pm update many` exists, but there is no CLI counterpart for creating items in bulk. An agent that needs to record N items has to spawn N `pm create` processes, and each one pays full startup plus workspace load. Typical cases:\n- importing findings or review threads;\n- mirroring notes into the tracker;\n- seeding a plan;\n- converting a spec into tasks.\n\n**Measured on 2026.10.13** (Linux, Node 26, a tracker of about 940 items growing to about 1,200):\n- 266 sequential `pm --json create --type <custom type> --title … --description … --body-file … --tags …` calls took **10 m 24 s wall time**, about 2.3 s per item.\n- 2 m 54 s of that was user CPU, so most of the cost is per-process startup and workspace reads, repeated 266 times.\n- The run is not atomic. An interruption leaves a partial import, and the caller has to build its own idempotency, for example by listing existing items and matching on a custom key field.\n\nThe SDK already has the primitive. #613 added a bulk multi-item atomic-mutation helper on top of `commitWorkspaceTransaction`. It just isn't reachable from the CLI.\n\n## Proposal\n`pm create many` (alias `pm create --from -`) reads JSONL or a JSON array from a file or stdin. Each record uses the same field names as `pm create` flags or their JSON equivalents, including custom fields, `body`, `tags`, dependencies and comments.\n- One process and one workspace transaction: all records are created or none, with history and merge receipts written as usual.\n- `--dry-run` validates every record first: types, required custom fields, dependency references (including references to other records in the same batch, through client-side `ref` keys), and duplicate-title detection against the existing tracker.\n- Optional idempotency key, `--upsert-by <field>`: an existing item whose field matches is updated instead of duplicated. That makes re-running an import safe.\n- The `--json` receipt lists created and updated ids in input order, mapped to each record's `ref`.\n\n## Why it matters for agents\nProject management is context management. The moments an agent wants to write many items at once (triage imports, review-thread capture, plan expansion) are exactly the moments a 2-3 s per-item cost and a non-atomic loop push it to skip tracking or to batch lossy summaries instead.\n\n\nIntake scope\n\nExtend the shipped atomic SDK/CLI mutation primitive with a discoverable bulk-create workflow, JSONL input and optional keyed upsert. The existing pm item mutate surface already commits ordered create/update/close batches and must be reused rather than reimplemented.\n\nDuplicate check: complete live all-status corpus2953 items with zero omissions/unreadable records plus request search/open/in-progress reads. Full live pm-xm7c and pm-y9hq are closed for shipped CLI/MCP and SDK atomic mutations; pm-xm7c history67 entries verifies current hash. No existing open JSONL/keyed-upsert owner was found. Current pm item mutate contract exposes required --transaction-id, --stdin-json and --dry-run; GH report incorrectly states no CLI bulk primitive. Remaining discoverability/JSONL/upsert request is additive."
